 "introductionText": "What FRUS answers directly: the series never uses the phrase soft power, but 25 documents from 1949 to 1978 show how the officials who ran American information work in East Asia and the Pacific described their message (contrast with communism, American strength, and after 1975 reassurance), their belief about the audience (nationalist, poor, wary of whites, persuaded by its own people, America-watching) and their judgement of effect (counts of output in the 1950s, doubt in the 1960s, surveys that moved with policy). Where FRUS points for the rest: the printed documents mostly came from the records of the U.S. Information Agency in Record Group 306 at the National Archives and from presidential libraries; the footnotes of the regional volumes cite unprinted State Department lot files and central files and presidential library files, kept as a separate channel. What this request cannot get from FRUS: any source that uses the term soft power, the audience's own voice, controlled measures of effect, or the private and cultural channels beyond the government; the Agency's own research reports are in the National Archives.",
